yes(522,'A','Propanal and propanone both have molecular formula C₃H₆O. They differ in connectivity and functional group: propanal is an aldehyde and propanone a ketone. Each other pair has different molecular formulae.',[CARB],options=['Propanal and propanone','Ethanoic acid and ethyl methanoate','Ethanoic acid and ethane-1,2-diol','2-Methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylbutane'],audit='Repaired clear compound-name spelling and removed next-year heading; independently counted molecular formulae.',check='isomer-formulae')
yes(523,'B','The reaction is 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. The 12 cm³ of oxygen consumes 24 cm³ of hydrogen and forms 24 cm³ of steam. Hydrogen remaining is 35 − 24 = 11 cm³. At 110 °C the total gas volume is 11 + 24 = 35 cm³, so hydrogen accounts for 11/35 × 100 = 31% to the nearest whole percent.',[RX,GAS],question='35 cm³ of hydrogen reacts completely with 12 cm³ of oxygen to produce steam at 110 °C and 760 mmHg. With all gas volumes compared at these conditions, what percentage of the final gas mixture is hydrogen?',check='steam-volume')
no(524,'Literal oxygen masses are0.33g and0.38g, giving O/Cu ratios0.130952 and0.25, a ratio1.90909 rather than2 within stated mass precision. The intended multiple-proportions illustration needs an intact numerical source; do not replace a mass or select the nearest integer.',answer='D intended, inconsistent supplied masses')
yes(525,'D','The horizontal section represents freezing at a constant temperature. In the usual cooling-curve interpretation this is characteristic of a pure substance, making a pure compound the best listed description. The graph alone does not identify its bonding or whether it is a hydrated salt.',[REB],question='A sample X, solid at room temperature, is melted, heated to 358 K and allowed to cool. Its temperature falls along OP, stays constant along the horizontal section PQ, and then falls again along QR. In the usual interpretation of this cooling curve, which description is supported by PQ?',options=['A mixture of salts','A hydrated salt','An ionic salt','A pure compound'],audit='Transcribed the complete qualitative PDF47 cooling curve and shared premise into accessible text; inference is conventional purity rather than proof of chemical identity.')
yes(526,'B','Vapour density relative to hydrogen is half the relative molecular mass. Therefore Mᵣ(XH₃) = 2 × 17.0 = 34.0. Subtracting the three hydrogen atoms gives Aᵣ(X) = 34.0 − 3 = 31.0.',[GAS],question='An element X forms a volatile hydride XH₃ with vapour density 17.0 relative to hydrogen. What is the relative atomic mass of X? [H = 1]',audit='Made the standard hydrogen reference explicit. Corrected old D to B without nearest-answer substitution.',check='hydride-mass')
yes(527,'C','By Graham’s law, rate is inversely proportional to the square root of molar mass. Time also increases with the volume required. Thus t = 7 × (60/30) × √(71/32) = 20.85 s, or 21 s to the nearest second.',[GAS],question='If 30 cm³ of oxygen diffuses through a porous plug in 7 s, how long will 60 cm³ of chlorine take under the same conditions? [O = 16, Cl = 35.5]',audit='Corrected old B to C. Restored standard atomic masses used in the independent Graham-law calculation.',check='graham-time')
yes(528,'C','Evaporation requires energy to overcome attractions between liquid molecules. When that energy is supplied by the body, the body loses thermal energy and cools.',[REB],question='A body cools when drops of liquid on it evaporate because',options=['Atmospheric vapour pressure has a cooling effect on the body','A temperature gradient exists between the drops and the body','The heat of vaporisation is drawn from the body','Random motion of liquid molecules causes cooling by itself'])
yes(529,'C','The configurations in C describe sodium and lithium. Each has one electron in its outermost shell and belongs to Group 1, so they have similar chemical properties. The other pairs have different outer-shell electron counts.',[REB],options=['1s²2s²2p⁵ and 1s²2s²2p⁴','1s²2s²2p⁴ and 1s²2s²2p⁶3s¹','1s²2s²2p⁶3s¹ and 1s²2s¹','1s²2s²2p⁴ and 1s²2s¹'],audit='Restored complete configurations and missing C boundary from PDF47.',check='valence-configurations')
yes(530,'D','Atomic radius generally decreases from left to right across a period because the increasing effective nuclear charge attracts electrons more strongly. It increases down a group as additional occupied electron shells are added.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-5-periodic-variations-in-element-properties'],question='Which property generally decreases across a period and increases down a group in the periodic table?',options=['Atomic number','Electron affinity','Ionisation energy','Atomic radius'])
yes(531,'C','P is sodium, which forms P⁺, and Q is oxygen, which forms Q²⁻ in the oxide. Two P⁺ ions balance one Q²⁻ ion, giving P₂Q. Thus x = 2 and y = 1.',[REB,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/13745?filter=oldest'],question='Two elements P and Q, with atomic numbers 11 and 8 respectively, combine to form PₓQᵧ. What are x and y respectively?',audit='Readable matching Myschool13745 full question explicitly confirms PₓQᵧ order and all four options. Corrected old B to C; independently solved ionic charge balance.',check='oxide-ratio')
yes(532,'B','The relative atomic mass is the abundance-weighted average: (16 × 0.90) + (18 × 0.10) = 16.2. The percentages describe the specified sample.',[REB],question='An oxygen sample contains isotopes ¹⁶₈O and ¹⁸₈O in abundances of 90% and 10% respectively. What is its relative atomic mass?',audit='Specified sample to avoid asserting these hypothetical abundances are natural oxygen composition.',check='isotope-average')
