yes(541,'B','Reaction enthalpy is the sum of product formation enthalpies minus the reactant sum. O₂ in its standard state contributes zero. Therefore ΔH = 2(−396) − 2(−297) = −198 kJ for the reaction as written.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/5-3-enthalpy'],question='For 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2SO₃(g), the standard formation enthalpies of SO₂ and SO₃ are −297 and −396 kJ mol⁻¹ respectively. What is the heat change for the reaction as written?',options=['−99 kJ','−198 kJ','+198 kJ','+683 kJ'],audit='Restored reaction arrow; expressed reaction-extent heat rather than ambiguous per-mole-of-product units.',check='hess-sulfur')
yes(542,'B','Use ΔG = ΔH − TΔS with consistent units. At 25 °C, T ≈ 298 K and ΔS = 0.0118 kJ mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Therefore ΔG = 89 − 298(0.0118) = 85.4836 kJ mol⁻¹, or 85.48 kJ mol⁻¹.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-4-free-energy','https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/13860'],question='For ½N₂(g) + ½O₂(g) → NO(g), ΔH° = 89 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS° = 11.8 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Calculate ΔG° at 25 °C, taking T = 298 K.',options=['88.71 kJ mol⁻¹','85.48 kJ mol⁻¹','−204.00 kJ mol⁻¹','−3427.40 kJ mol⁻¹'],audit='Readable matching Myschool13860 confirms missing NO(g) product and all options. Restored entropy K⁻¹ dimension, independently recalculated and corrected old A to B.',check='gibbs-energy')
yes(543,'C','The overall reaction order is the sum of the exponents of concentration in the experimentally determined rate law. For rate = k[X]ⁿ[Y]ᵐ, the order is n + m.',[REB],question='If a reaction has rate law rate = k[X]ⁿ[Y]ᵐ, what is its overall order?',options=['nm','n/m','n + m','n − m'],audit='Restored missing fraction denominator in B from PDF48.')
