yes(550,'A','Sulfate ions form a white precipitate of barium sulfate with Ba²⁺: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s). Acidifying with dilute nitric acid helps exclude interfering carbonate precipitates.',[RX,REB],question='Which reaction is used in the laboratory test for sulfate ions?',options=['SO₄²⁻(aq) + Ba²⁺(aq) → BaSO₄(s), in dilute HNO₃','Cu(s) + 4H⁺(aq) + 2SO₄²⁻(aq) → CuSO₄(s) + 2H₂O(l) + SO₂(g)','4H⁺(aq) + 2SO₄²⁻(aq) + 2e⁻ → SO₄²⁻(aq) + 2H₂O(l) + SO₂(g)','CuO(s) + 2H⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → CuSO₄(aq) + H₂O(l)'],audit='Restored charges/arrows in every option from PDF49; retained source distractor phases.',check='barium-sulfate')
